{"type": "thinking", "text": "Interesting! The reschedule switch is ON (delay inputs are shown: again 60, hard 600, good 0), even though I toggled it off before Build. Wait, I tapped [31] once, but observe before Build still showed the delays hidden (only [31] switch and [32] show excluded) — actually the pre-build observe after toggle showed element [31] switch and [32] button, no delay inputs, meaning reschedule was OFF. But after Build, the deck options show reschedule ON with delays. That's a bug! The \"Reschedule cards based on my answers in this deck\" setting was not persisted when creating the filtered deck; it reverted to ON.\n\nHold on — maybe tapping the switch toggled it off, but then I tapped Build; the deck options show it checked. That suggests the toggle didn't stick, or Build ignored it. Is this the seeded bug? The changed area is \"criteria dropdown lists every selection criterion\", not reschedule. But maybe it's a pre-existing behavior: \"New cards only\" filtered decks always reschedule? Hmm. Actually, for a filtered deck with search `is:new`, reschedule might default ON because new cards need rescheduling? No, the user toggled off; it should persist.\n\nWait, maybe I toggled the switch off, but the delay fields hidden state in the pre-build observe — compare first builder observe: [13] switch reschedule checked, [14] allow empty switch. After typing filter and limit, observe showed [31] switch and [32] show excluded, with no delay inputs. Hmm, but that might be because the form was scrolled? No, the observe lists all visible elements; the screen scrolled down when keyboard opened maybe, hiding delay inputs below. Actually after typing limit, the screen layout shifted and the \"Create/update even if empty\" switch [14] was missing too, and \"Show excluded\" was at 1436. With the keyboard open, the content was scrolled; delay inputs might exist but hidden by keyboard? Hmm, the observe after toggle [31] still showed no delay inputs and no allow-empty switch. So reschedule off? Or just out of view.\n\nLet me check the current deck options: reschedule switch [13] checked per observe (no [checked] flag though; only switches list \"id=... [clickable,checked]\" for first builder's [13]). Here [13] has no checked attribute shown, but the delay inputs are present, which implies reschedule is ON (they only show when on). Hmm, maybe the delay inputs are always present but greyed? In the first builder, reschedule was on and delays weren't shown in observe (first builder had elements up to [15] show excluded and no delay inputs!). Wait, the first builder observe (FAB create) showed:\n[11] switch second filter\n[12] Options label\n[13] switch reschedule [checked]\n[14] switch allow empty\n[15] button show excluded\nNo delay inputs. Hmm, but when I later opened the previous filtered deck options (from custom study), it showed delay inputs [14]-[20], with reschedule switch [13] not checked. Wait, that filtered deck was \"don't reschedule\" (All cards random don't reschedule) and had delay inputs visible. So delay inputs show even when reschedule is off. So presence doesn't imply on.\n\nThus, whether the switch persisted isn't visible in observe. Let me screenshot the deck options now to see the switch state and compare with the builder before Build. Hmm, but the builder state before Build is gone. I did toggle it once though. Let me re-examine the pre-build observe numbering: after toggling, elements were same, no checked attr. Before toggling, [13] was \"Switch ... [clickable,checked]\" (from first builder observe). In the post-toggle observe, [31] was \"Switch ... [clickable]\" without checked—suggesting unchecked! But that observe was after typing the limit and keyboard open; accessibility \"checked\" might be omitted for switches in that state? In the very first builder observe, [13] showed \"[clickable,checked]\" — so checked is exposed when checked. After toggle, [31] shows \"[clickable]\" → unchecked. So toggle worked in the builder.\n\nNow in the resulting deck options, [13] \"Switch ... [clickable]\" without checked → unchecked! So reschedule is OFF, correct. The delay inputs are visible regardless. Good, no bug. My mistake.\n\nSo filter, limit, sort persisted correctly: \"deck:Spanish is:new\", 4, Order added. Good.\n\nNow test \"Rebuild\" after modifying the deck's filter? And \"Show any excluded cards\". Hmm.\n\nGiven time, let me now focus on verifying the filtered deck criteria mapping more: create custom study decks for \"Due cards only\" and \"All review cards in random order\" and check filter strings. But those would create empty decks (no due/review cards) and may error. Hmm, since French has 3 new cards and Spanish 4 new. No review cards. So due/review criteria produce empty decks. Maybe the builder shows a message \"No cards matched\" instead of creating. That's expected. Can we still inspect the filter? No deck created.\n\nBut could test on the current state: with the filtered deck exists, cards are review cards inside it? The filtered deck has 4 cards with reschedule off but they're \"in\" the filtered deck as review due to don't-reschedule. Could custom study \"All review cards in random order\" from... the filtered deck? Hmm, filtered decks can't have custom study? They have \"Rebuild\". Not worth.\n\nAlternatively, test criteria mapping on French after reviewing a card to create a review card. That changes scheduling; we could review one French card (normal study) then grade Good; it becomes review/learning. Actually after first review, new card goes to learning, then graduating -> review after intervals. That's extra complexity.\n\nMaybe the criteria dropdown bug is simpler: the labels. All four options are correct labels. The truncated last one is visible: \"All cards in random order (don't res..\" in the popup. The full text appears in the field after selection. Is truncation a \"cut_off\" bug? The screen truncates the option in the dropdown, so a user can't distinguish \"don't reschedule\" from a hypothetical \"reschedule\" variant; but they can select it and read the full text in the field. It's minor but real and within \"criteria dropdown\" area. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe there IS a fifth option expected: \"Preview new cards\"? Let me examine the strings by checking the app's language? Can't read code. But maybe the \"Custom study\" menu had \"Preview new cards\" as a separate function which creates a filtered deck with `is:new` and a limit; not a criterion.\n\nLet's reconsider: The task says changed: \"Filtered deck builder: the criteria dropdown now lists every selection criterion the scheduler offers.\" Maybe the bug is that the dropdown now lists criteria **in a wrong order** or **duplicated** or **missing one that exists in the new scheduler**. Anki's newer scheduler (2.1.57+) added \"Preview new cards\"? Hmm.\n\nHmm, I wonder if the criteria dropdown in this build is missing **\"All cards in random order (reschedule)\"**? Wait, the \"don't reschedule\" variant is one criterion. Another criterion in Anki's custom study is \"All cards in random order (don't reschedule)\" only. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me check AnkiDroid source strings from memory. I recall `StudyByCardStateDialog` labels are:\n- `custom_study_new_card` = \"New cards only\"\n- `custom_study_due_card` = \"Due cards only\"\n- `custom_study_review_card` = \"All review cards in random order\"\n- `custom_study_all_card` = \"All cards in random order (don't reschedule)\"\n\nThere's a `StudyByCardStateDialog` with `CardState` enum values: NEW_CARDS, DUE_CARDS, REVIEW_CARDS, ALL_CARDS. 4.\n\nSo the dropdown is complete. Maybe the seeded filtered-deck bug lies elsewhere in the filtered deck area. Let's test:\n- Filtered deck builder with empty/invalid filter and Build: does it handle errors?\n- Filtered deck \"Rebuild\" behavior.\n- \"Empty\" action.\n- Filtered deck study: cards automatically returned after review? Maybe.\n\nThe area is \"filtered decks\", so all aspects. We tested creation via custom study and FAB, counts, filter, sort, limit, reschedule, delete. Remaining: Build with no matches, invalid input, empty filter, second filter, and rebuild.\n\nPotential seeded bug: the builder creates the deck even when empty despite \"Create/update this deck even if empty\" being off, or crashes. Or ", "truncated": true}
