{"type": "thinking", "text": "Interesting! \"All review cards in random order\" produced filter \"-is:new deck:French\", NOT \"is:review deck:French\"! Hmm. That's semantically equivalent? \"-is:new\" includes cards that are not new: could include review AND learning cards, and also suspended? Hmm, Anki's custom study for \"All review cards\" might use \"-is:new\" (all non-new, including learning). Is that correct? The option says \"All review cards in random order\" — should arguably be \"is:review\". But \"-is:new\" includes learning cards too. Since there are no learning cards, observed 1 (bonjour review) correct.\n\nHmm, is that a bug? The criteria dropdown says \"All review cards in random order\" but the filter includes learning cards (non-new). The scheduler's \"review cards\" criterion is `is:review`. Using `-is:new` is broader. This could be the seeded bug! The changed note: \"criteria dropdown now lists every selection criterion the scheduler offers.\" Maybe the bug relates to criteria mapping; here \"All review cards\" maps to \"-is:new\" instead of \"is:review\". Would the developer consider \"-is:new\" acceptable? In AnkiDroid's custom study code, \"All review cards\" historically uses `-is:new` (I recall `CustomStudyDialog` using `\"deck:%s -is:new\"`). Hmm! Actually yes: in Anki desktop custom study, \"All review cards in random order\" search is \"-is:new\" maybe because \"review\" in the old sense = non-new. Hmm.\n\nLet me recall Anki's custom study: The \"Study by card state or tag\" options in Anki: New cards only, Due cards only, All review cards in random order, All cards in random order (don't reschedule). In Anki's `customstudy.py`, the search for review cards is `deck:\"...\" -is:new` I think. Actually the code:\n```\ndef _get_study_by_card_state_query(deck, state):\n    if state == NEW: return f'deck:\"{deck}\" is:new'\n    elif state == DUE: return f'deck:\"{deck}\" is:due'\n    elif state == REVIEW: return f'deck:\"{deck}\" -is:new'\n    ...\n```\nHmm plausibly yes. So \"-is:new\" is intended.\n\nSo no bug there.\n\nNow test \"Due cards only\": expected \"is:due deck:French\" and no cards. Let's do it: delete this deck, then create Due cards deck. Since bonjour is due in 4 days, `is:due` should match no cards → error. If it matches bonjour, bug.\n\nDelete deck first. Back to deck list."}
